Here's a simple PyQt5 GUI program that creates a window with a button:

```python
# Import required modules from PyQt5
from PyQt5.QtWidgets import (QApplication, QMainWindow, QWidget, QPushButton)
from PyQt5.QtCore import Qt
import sys

# Create the main application instance
app = QApplication(sys.argv)

# Create the main window
window = QMainWindow()

# Set the window title
window.setWindowTitle("Click Here")

# Create a button widget
button = QWidget()
button.setFixedSize(100, 30)
button.move(50, 50)
button QPushButton
button.setText("Click Here")
button.setIcon(from icons import StartBtn)  # You need to have an icon file in your project

# Add some spacing between the button and window title
space = QWidget()
space.setFixedSize(200, 10)
space.move(50, 160)

# Create a QVBoxLayout for layout management
layout = QVBoxLayout(window)
layout.addWidget(button)
layout.addStretch()  # Adds vertical space
layout.addWidget(space)
window.setLayout(layout)

# Connect the button's click event to print "Hello"
button.clicked.pyqtSlot().emit("clicked").connect(lambda: print("Hello, World!"))

# Show the window and run the application
window.show()
app.exec_()
```

This code creates a simple GUI with:
1. A window titled "Click Here"
2. A button labeled "Click Here" at the bottom of the window
3. A small amount of spacing between the button and the title

When you click the button, it will print "Hello, World!" to the console.

Note: This code assumes that you have an icon file named `StartBtn` in your project's icons directory. If you don't have such a file, you'll need to either:
1. Use a different icon file
2. Add an emoji or checkmark instead of an icon

To run this program, make sure you have PyQt5 installed and the application will automatically start when you run it.

You can modify the size of the button by changing `button.setFixedSize(100, 30)`, adjust its position with `button.move(50, 50)`, and change the spacing values to match your preferences.
